<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mudan River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mudanjiang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mudan River is a river of north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, about 700 kilometres long, rising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jilin">Jilin province</a> and flowing north through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heilongjiang">Heilongjiang</a> to join the Songhua. Its valley was the heartland of Balhae, whose Upper Capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanggyeong">Sanggyeong</a> stood near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ning-an">Ning'an</a>, and it later lay in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a>. The city of Mudanjiang is named after it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mudan-river.jpg" alt="Mudan River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bosiota · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MDJ_vista_sul_fiume.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
